---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import QrCodeGenerator from '@/components/tools/generators/QrCodeGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="qr-code-generator">
  <QrCodeGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to make a QR code</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type or paste what the code must contain. A URL is the usual case, and any text works.</li>
      <li>Set the export size. The PNG has exactly that many pixels; the SVG scales to any size.</li>
      <li>Choose the colours. Keep the dark modules dark: the contrast readout tells you when they are too light.</li>
      <li>Raise the error-correction level if you add a logo, or if the printed code can get scuffed.</li>
      <li>Download the PNG for screens and documents, or the SVG for print and anything that must scale.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The QR code generator makes the symbol in this tab and draws it on a canvas, so there is no rate
      limit and no watermark. The code holds your text directly, with no redirect service in the middle,
      so it cannot expire or start to show an advert later.
    </p>

    <h2>What you can put in a QR code</h2>
    <p>
      Phone cameras recognise some URI schemes and act on them. They show all other content as plain
      text.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>What you want</th>
            <th>What to encode</th>
            <th>What the phone does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Open a web page</td><td><code>https://example.com/page</code></td><td>Offers to open the link</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Call a number</td><td><code>tel:+15551234567</code></td><td>Opens the dialler with the number filled in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Send an email</td><td><code>mailto:hi@example.com?subject=Hello&amp;body=Hi</code></td><td>Opens a pre-filled draft</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Send a text</td><td><code>SMSTO:+15551234567:Message</code></td><td>Opens the messaging app</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Show a location</td><td><code>geo:48.858370,2.294481</code></td><td>Opens the map at that point</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Start a video call</td><td><code>https://meet.example.com/room</code></td><td>Opens the meeting link</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Plain text</td><td><code>Table 14</code></td><td>Displays the text</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Error correction in a QR code</h2>
    <p>
      A QR code stores its data with Reed–Solomon error correction, the same family of codes used on
      compact discs and in deep-space telemetry. Redundant data spread through the symbol lets a decoder
      rebuild the original when part of it is unreadable. You choose how much redundancy to use:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Level</th>
            <th>Recoverable</th>
            <th>Capacity cost</th>
            <th>Use it for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>L</td><td>~7%</td><td>Smallest symbol</td><td>Clean screens, long payloads such as vCards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>M</td><td>~15%</td><td>Slightly larger</td><td>Default for most printing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Q</td><td>~25%</td><td>Noticeably denser</td><td>Labels, packaging, anything that gets handled</td></tr>
          <tr><td>H</td><td>~30%</td><td>Densest</td><td>Codes with a centre logo, industrial environments</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Redundancy costs space: at level H the same text needs a physically larger symbol than at level L.
      A dense, high-correction code printed small can scan worse than a sparse, low-correction one,
      because the camera cannot resolve the individual modules. Pick the lowest level that survives your
      environment.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a logo without breaking the code</h2>
    <p>
      A centre logo works because the decoder treats it as damage and repairs it. Four rules follow:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>At level H, cover at most about 28% of the symbol width. More than that is more than the redundancy can rebuild.</li>
      <li>Cover the centre, never a corner. The three large squares are finder patterns and the small one is an alignment pattern. If you cover one, the scanner cannot find the code.</li>
      <li>Put a solid plate behind the logo. It gives the decoder a clean damaged region in place of a half-visible module grid.</li>
      <li>Test on real phones, because decoders differ. Before a print run, scan the final artwork with an iPhone camera, an Android camera and one third-party scanner app.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Choosing a QR code size for print</h2>
    <p>
      As a rule of thumb, a QR code must be about one tenth of the distance it is scanned from. It also
      needs a quiet zone of four modules of blank space on each side, or the decoder cannot tell where the
      symbol ends.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Scanning distance</th>
            <th>Minimum symbol size</th>
            <th>Typical use</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10 cm</td><td>1 cm</td><td>Product label, ticket stub</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30 cm</td><td>3 cm</td><td>Business card, menu, packaging</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 m</td><td>10 cm</td><td>Table tent, shop window, flyer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3 m</td><td>30 cm</td><td>Poster, exhibition stand</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10 m</td><td>1 m</td><td>Billboard, building wrap</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Export the SVG when the code goes to print. It is a few vector paths, so the module edges stay sharp
      at any size and do not get the soft grey halo of a bitmap scaled up in a layout program.
    </p>

    <h2>Colour, contrast and materials</h2>
    <p>
      Scanners convert the image to black and white before they decode it, so they read the luminance
      difference and ignore hue. Pale yellow on white has plenty of colour contrast and almost no luminance
      contrast, and it will not scan. Aim for a ratio of at least 4:1, with the dark modules darker than the
      background. Inverted codes fail on many Android devices and on nearly all laser-based scanners.
    </p>
    <p>
      The surface matters as much as the colour. Glossy laminates reflect the camera flash, curved bottles
      distort the module grid, and matte dark paper reduces the contrast. On a difficult surface, raise the
      error-correction level and print the code larger.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
